{
"dur": 59.565,
"shots": [
{
"i": 1,
"start": 0.0,
"vo_start": 0.6,
"vo_end": 6.902,
"text": "In March 2000 he got his chance: chief executive of Bank One in Chicago, a big bank in poor shape.",
"end": 7.177
},
{
"i": 2,
"start": 7.177,
"vo_start": 7.452,
"vo_end": 15.412,
"text": "He bought about two million Bank One shares with his own money, close to sixty million dollars. He later said it was about half of everything he had.",
"end": 15.687
},
{
"i": 3,
"start": 15.687,
"vo_start": 15.962,
"vo_end": 22.432,
"text": "Then he went through the bank line by line. He cut costs, cleaned up bad loans and tightened the checks on risk.",
"end": 22.707
},
{
"i": 4,
"start": 22.707,
"vo_start": 22.982,
"vo_end": 26.137,
"text": "Within a few years, Bank One was making billions a year again.",
"end": 26.412
},
{
"i": 5,
"start": 26.412,
"vo_start": 26.687,
"vo_end": 37.087,
"text": "In January 2004, JPMorgan Chase agreed to buy Bank One for about fifty-eight billion dollars. Part of the deal was that Dimon would one day run the combined bank.",
"end": 37.362
},
{
"i": 6,
"start": 37.362,
"vo_start": 37.637,
"vo_end": 44.637,
"text": "He became president, and on the last day of 2005 he took over as chief executive. A year later he was chairman too.",
"end": 44.912
},
{
"i": 7,
"start": 44.912,
"vo_start": 45.187,
"vo_end": 52.146,
"text": "His rule was simple. Hold more capital than rivals, keep piles of cash ready, and plan for the worst year, not the average one.",
"end": 52.421
},
{
"i": 8,
"start": 52.421,
"vo_start": 52.696,
"vo_end": 58.615,
"text": "He called it a fortress balance sheet. To see why that mattered, you need to know how a bank actually makes money.",
"end": 59.565
}
],
